    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for that. i noticed something though—the originalist section you wrote uses specific legal tests and mentions structural constraints on federal power, whereas the living constitutionalist section seems to focus more on general philosophical concepts like \"judgment.\" \n\ngiven that current Supreme Court jurisprudence, especially post-Heller and Bruen, has leaned heavily toward originalism, wouldn't it be more accurate to say that originalism is currently the dominant and most legally rigorous methodology used by the Court, rather than treating them as two methods on equal footing? it feels like the living constitutionalist approach is being presented as a secondary or more subjective viewpoint compared to the formalist structure of originalism.",
